<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Section 1: Review of Previous Concepts</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 1: Review of Previous Concepts</h3>
<p><strong>Quick Summary</strong></p>
<p>Throughout the course, we have used the preposition <code>l-</code>
(ܠ) to mark the direct object of a verb. For example, <em>qṭal
l-malkā</em> (he killed the king) or <em>qṭal l-ēh</em> (he killed him).
We also learned how to attach pronominal suffixes to nouns and
prepositions to indicate possession or indirect objects
(<em>bayt-eh</em> = his house; <em>l-ēh</em> = to him).</p>
<p><strong>5-Question Warm-Up Drill</strong></p>
<p><em>(Answers in Section 9)</em></p>
<ol type="1">
<li>Translate: <em>Nēthūn</em> (from <em>ʾ-t-y</em>, they will
come).</li>
<li>What happens to the noun <em>malkā</em> when you add the suffix for
“my”?</li>
<li>Translate: <em>ʾEqṭūl</em>.</li>
<li>How do you say “to us” using the preposition <em>l-</em>?</li>
<li>How does Syriac traditionally mark a definite direct object?</li>
</ol>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
