<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 11 · Section 10: Grammatical Summary</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 10: Grammatical Summary</h3>
<p><strong>THE VERBAL SUFFIX PARADIGM (He killed…)</strong></p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">English</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Suffix on verb</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…me</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-anī</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlanī (he killed me)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…you(m)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-āḵ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlāḵ (he killed you)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…you(f)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-ēky</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlēky (he killed you)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…him</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-eh</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭleh (he killed him)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…her</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-āh</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlāh (he killed her)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…us</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-an</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlan (he killed us)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;">…them(m)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-hūn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">qaṭlhūn (he killed them)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>DOUBLE DETERMINATION RULE</strong></p>
<p>Sometimes Syriac uses the verb suffix AND the preposition
<code>l-</code> in the same sentence. This is called <em>prolepsis</em>
or double determination, used for clarity or emphasis. <em>Qaṭl-eh
l-malkā</em> = He killed him, [namely] the king.</p>

]]></text></questiontext></question>
